  "what": "The refined check after Joel's correction (2026-10-03 21:18 UTC): his P4 and P5 do break the march (\"or however you'd actually say that\"; \"Wait and listen. If nothing comes, that's fine for now\"; stopping to consider what something might feel like; \"just listen\"), so a reader labels each sentence a step (S) or a break (B), with his kinds of break in the definition (the prompt's examples are new wording, so his sentences aren't in it). It's reviewer.py march / march-score now.",
  "readers": "two Claude Sonnet runs over the same 100 calibration paragraphs (50 PASS, 50 FAIL, single paragraphs of 40 to 220 words, seed 11, his P4, P5, the altered-states tries and SWS P1's pair included), in two shuffled orders, five batches of 20 each, nothing but the prompt file open",
  "prompt": "Below are N paragraphs from a friendly self-help article about inner-child work, each one on its own, with its sentences numbered. In this article \"your little one\" is the inner child.\n\nLabel every numbered sentence of every paragraph S (STEP) or B (BREAK):\n- S, a step: the sentence moves the paragraph one step further along a sequence, the way a manual or a program does: the next instruction, the next fact or case, a rule, a reason, or a condition or caution attached to a step.\n- B, a break: the sentence stops that forward march, even for a moment. For example: the writer reacts or exclaims; an aside that loosens what was just said (\"Or put it in your own words.\"); a pause, waiting, or a permission (\"If it doesn't come, that's okay for today.\"); stopping to consider what something might feel like or be like (\"It might be more of a mood. Sadness, maybe.\"); just staying with something, like listening or being present, instead of doing the next thing; a story or memory; a joke; a question; the writer's own doubt or opinion stepping back from the sequence.\nJudge what the sentence does in its paragraph, not its grammar: an instruction to wait or just listen can be a break, and a plain statement can be a step.\n\nDon't judge whether the writing is good or whether it sounds like AI, and don't guess where a paragraph came from. Return only one line per paragraph, one letter per numbered sentence, in order, like this:\nT1: S S B S S\nT2: S B S B\n",
